arXiv:2607.10501v2 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: We prove a sharp extrapolation bound for $k$-sparse Fourier sums with arbitrary real frequencies and no separation assumption. For every $g(t)=\sum_{j=1}^k v_je^{i\lambda_jt}$ with $\lambda_j\in\mathbb{R}$ and every $x\geq 1$, \begin{align*}
  |g(x)|\le k\exp(O(k\operatorname{arcosh} x))\|g\|_{L^2[-1,1]}. \end{align*} Near the endpoint at $x=1+\delta$, this gives an exponential factor of $\exp(O(k\sqrt\delta))$, improving on the $\exp(O(k^2\log k\cdot\delta))$ estimate of Chen and Price (ICALP 2019), and the exponent is optimal up to a constant factor. Our theorem has algorithmic applications to recovering gapless Fourier-sparse signals: it improves the resolution of cluster-location algorithms and the sample complexity of Fourier interpolation algorithms. In addition, it gives sharp bounds on the error transfer of sparse Fourier models just outside the training interval.

arXiv:2610.03982v2 Announce Type: replace-cross Abstract: We study constant-delay enumeration for conjunctive queries with negation ($	exttt{CQ}^{
eg}$). It is known that $	extit{signed-acyclic}$ queries can be enumerated with constant delay after linear preprocessing time, using linked lists with skip links. We use a hypergraph width measure for $	exttt{CQ}^{
eg}$, the $	extit{fractional signed hyperorder width}$ ($	extsf{sfhow}$), which requires a single variable order to simultaneously handle every subset of the negative atoms, which Capelli et al. introduced to characterize the preprocessing time of direct access. $	extsf{sfhow}$ strictly generalizes signed-acyclicity (recovered when $	extsf{sfhow} = 1$) and fractional hypertree width (recovered on queries without negation). Our main algorithmic result is a variable-elimination algorithm that achieves constant-delay enumeration on input $I$ for any full $	exttt{CQ}^{
eg}$ query with preprocessing time $O(|I|^{	extsf{sfhow}})$. Furthermore, we show that using multiple variable orders can improve this bound, exhibiting an $O(|I|^{3/2})$ algorithm for $k$-cycle queries with at least one negative edge.
